+### Security
+
+We are going to use encryption tls 1.3 for on wire communication. TLS 1.3 provides more secure cipher suites then tls 1.2. We can control the communication to use tls 1.3 as both client and server are shipped by us. 
+
+We are going to use signed **X.509 certificates** from CA. The CA can be self hosted as well, but should be
+available in truststore for both client and server. Will refer X.509 certificate  as certificate throughout the document.
+
+
+#### Authentication
+
+We will rely on the mTLS for identity validation. In production, the certificates presented by server and client will be short lived to reduce security issues for stale certificcates. These certificates will be genrated through automation and delivered though agents, but in this assignment it will be manual generated and signed by CA. We will rely on certificate CN/SAN to validate the identity.
+
+There are other mechanism to authenticate like secret key to sign the request and then use the passed signature and access key to validate the caller by invoking identity management like how it is done with/in AWS services. It adds an external dependency.
+
+We will rely on grpc code in case Authentication errors.
+
+#### Authorization
+
+We will use the extension from the certificate to embed the roles.  We can use below roles for this assignment :-
+
+- `JobExecuteRead`: User can start/stop/status/stream it's own job.
+- `JobGlobalRead`: User can status/stream everyone's job.
+- `JobGlobalExecuteRead`: User can stop/status/stream job from other user's + start it's own job.
+
+And
+
+```Structure for Extension
+Custom Extension:
+  OID: 1.3.6.1.4.1.12345.1.1
+  Critical: False
+  Value: encodeHex({"JobExecuteRead", "JobGlobalExecuteRead"})
+```
+
+In the presence of multiple roles, role with highest privilge will be choosen. We will rely on grpc code in case of Authorization errors.
+
+### Implementation
+
+#### Validation
+Provide basic validation to check if the resources mentioned in job can be fullfilled by the system. Like requested cpu/memory shouldn't be greater than overall system resources.
+
+For production systems, the validation should be against available resources which requires resource tracking (Not in the scope of the assignment). 
+
+
+#### Storage
+
+- For this assignment, job related metadata will be stored on disk in files. The structure will look like `<base_directory>/<job_id>/job.metadata`
+It will help us to resist against the service crashes in recovering job started through service. 
+
+- The output from commands will be redirected to disk under log folder`<base_directory>/<job_id>/logs/` using the service instead of using underlying linux redirection to prevent process from hanging. Possible solutions in case the disk gets filled
+  - Truncate logs based on max logSizePerJob.
+  - Track the disk size , once configured `watermark` is hit, start truncating logs based on usage.
+  - Track the disk size , once configured `watermark` is hit, delete the job logs and start fresh.
+  For this assignment, log truncation is not considered to be part of solution. But it is required for production to have static stability and prevent outages. These logs will be the source for streaming job ouput.
+
+
+Also the disk writes for command logging will happen in service, without impacting the disk io limits of the job itself.
+
+#### Applying limits and consistency
+
+Limits on the process will be applied using cgroup. Below is file under which cgroups will be created for jobs spawned by service.
+
+```
+/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu/jobworker/<job_id>/*
+/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/jobworker/<job_id>/*
+/sys/fs/cgroup/blkio/jobworker/<job_id>/*
+```
+
+It is important to create these files before with limits already in place and add process id to the 
+`cgroup.procs`. As we will not know the process id before spawing the process, better way is to create a dummy process which will add itself to `cgroup.procs` and spawn the command for execution.
